# Cloudflare Pages headers file.
#
# Masi's web build uses dart2wasm + drift's OPFS (Origin Private File
# System) worker. Both require the page to be "cross-origin isolated", which
# only happens when the top-level document is served with COOP + COEP. See:
# https://web.dev/articles/coop-coep
# https://drift.simonbinder.eu/web/#cross-origin-isolation
#
# `require-corp` is stricter than `credentialless` but this app has no
# cross-origin embeds that need the relaxed mode, so pick the safer default.
#
# Cloudflare Pages also needs an explicit content-type for *.wasm — without
# it some edge configs serve wasm as application/octet-stream, which some
# browsers refuse to `instantiateStreaming()`.
#
# Anti-stale-shell reload fix (#55): Flutter's default cleanup service worker
# (`flutter_service_worker.js`) calls `client.navigate(client.url)` on
# activate for any returning visitor still holding an old caching SW — a
# real, user-visible reload. Without an explicit Cache-Control, Cloudflare's
# default heuristic caching lets a stale `flutter_bootstrap.js`/`index.html`/
# `main.dart.wasm` etc. linger in the browser/edge cache, so a returning
# visitor's shell and app code disagree and this reload-reconciliation dance
# repeats. `no-cache` (NOT `no-store`, and deliberately NOT `immutable` —
# Flutter does not content-hash these filenames per build, so `immutable`
# would pin stale code forever) means "cache it, but always revalidate via
# ETag first": a cheap 304 when nothing changed, always-fresh the moment a
# new build lands. Applied via the blanket `/*` block below (same block that
# sets COOP/COEP) rather than enumerating every shell/asset path individually
# — it's simpler, and everything under this build EXCEPT the two
# hand-versioned drift/sqlite assets called out further down is unversioned,
# so this blanket rule is still the right default for everything else.

/*
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin
  Cross-Origin-Embedder-Policy: require-corp
  Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy: same-origin
  Cache-Control: no-cache

/*.wasm
  Content-Type: application/wasm

/canvaskit/*.wasm
  Content-Type: application/wasm

# Long-cache the drift/sqlite web-worker assets. Unlike the shell files
# above, these two are NOT emitted per Flutter build -- they're hand-copied
# into web/ and version-pinned against the locked `drift` package version in
# `web/.drift_asset_versions` (checked by `tool/build_web.sh`'s asset-drift
# gate), so a content change always comes with a version bump + redeploy
# rather than a same-URL silent swap. That makes them safe to mark
# `immutable`, unlike `main.dart.wasm`/`main.dart.js` (deliberately NOT
# covered here -- those filenames are unversioned per build, so caching them
# immutably would reintroduce the #55 stale-shell bug the `/*` block above
# fixes). Cloudflare Pages resolves same-named headers by path specificity,
# not file order, so these override the `/*` block's `no-cache` for exactly
# these two paths while every other header from `/*` (COOP/COEP/CORP) still
# applies.
/sqlite3.wasm
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

/drift_worker.js
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# The service worker itself must ALWAYS be revalidated. `sw.js` is how a new
# build reaches an existing installation: `tool/gen_sw_manifest.dart` stamps a
# content-derived SHELL_VERSION into it at build time, the byte change is what
# makes the browser treat the worker as updated, and its install then
# precaches the new shell. A long-cached sw.js would freeze a user on one
# shell version indefinitely — the exact "stranded on a half-updated shell"
# failure the offline work exists to prevent. The blanket `/*` block above
# already sets `no-cache`; this restates it explicitly and non-negotiably so a
# future caching sweep cannot fold sw.js in with the `immutable` assets above
# by accident. Browsers additionally cap service-worker script caching at 24h
# regardless, but 24h of staleness is still 24h too many for a rollover path.
/sw.js
  Cache-Control: no-cache
